<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  THE TRANSPORT READOUT'S WIDTH DOES NOT FOLLOW THE PLAYHEAD.

  Reported by the author: "the bar right below the menu is jittering again as
  the positions change", and what moves is the contents sliding sideways.
  QBX-215 gave the readout fixed-width DIGITS, which keeps the glyphs still but
  not the LABEL: "9.4.959" is nine characters and "10.1.000" is ten, so the
  label's hint grew by one character the moment the playhead crossed bar 10 and
  the tempo box, the master meter, the latency readout and every toolbar
  sharing that row moved with it. Measured in the running app, through this
  very seam: hint 65 px up to bar 9, 73 px from bar 10.

  WHY THE ASSERTION IS A BOOLEAN AND NOT A PIXEL COUNT. A test run never
  show()s a window (main.cpp says so), so no widget is ever mapped and its
  rectangle describes nothing; and a px figure is a font-and-DPI number that
  could not be asserted on another box. `posPinned=1` is the invariant itself:
  the position label's own hint FITS inside the width it reserved, so the width
  the layout gives it is that reserved CONSTANT whatever the playhead says.

  THE FOUR POSITIONS ARE THE FOUR DIGIT COUNTS, which is the whole point — one
  position could not tell a reserved width from a measured one. 120 BPM,
  48 kHz, PPQ 960, 4/4: 25 frames to the tick, 96000 to the bar.

      frame        bar.beat.tick    bar digits
      0            1.1.000          1
      912500       10.3.020         2
      9600000      101.1.000        3
      95904000     1000.1.000       4

  The whole line is asserted, not just the flag: a build that pinned the width
  by truncating the text would satisfy the flag alone.

  WATCHED FAILING, twice. With the label's setMinimumWidth() removed all five
  assertions fail; with the reservation cut to one bar digit the three above
  bar 9 fail and the two at bar 1 pass. The meter half of the same report is
  gated in levelmeter_ticks_test's testPinnedLength().

  Bar 10000 is deliberately NOT here. The reservation is four digits and the
  label is allowed to grow past it (a minimum, not a fixed width — eliding the
  bar number would be worse than one more move), so a fifth digit is the
  documented exception and asserting it would gate the wrong thing.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="transport_readout_width_is_stable">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <add-track index="-1"/>

    <!-- ONE BAR DIGIT: the state the readout starts in. -->
    <set-locator position="0"/>
    <assert-transport-readout
        contains="pos=▶ 1.1.000|sel=[ — ]|mode=bars|selFrames=-|posPinned=1"/>

    <!-- TWO: the crossing that moved the toolbar in the report. -->
    <set-locator position="912500"/>
    <assert-transport-readout
        contains="pos=▶ 10.3.020|sel=[ — ]|mode=bars|selFrames=-|posPinned=1"/>

    <!-- THREE. -->
    <set-locator position="9600000"/>
    <assert-transport-readout
        contains="pos=▶ 101.1.000|sel=[ — ]|mode=bars|selFrames=-|posPinned=1"/>

    <!-- FOUR: the edge of the reservation, still pinned. -->
    <set-locator position="95904000"/>
    <assert-transport-readout
        contains="pos=▶ 1000.1.000|sel=[ — ]|mode=bars|selFrames=-|posPinned=1"/>

    <!-- ...AND BACK, because a reservation that only ever grew would also pass
         every assertion above: the label must not shrink to the short reading
         either, or the toolbar slides back the way it came. -->
    <set-locator position="0"/>
    <assert-transport-readout
        contains="pos=▶ 1.1.000|sel=[ — ]|mode=bars|selFrames=-|posPinned=1"/>
  </actions>
</SActionScript>
